          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have had a dry cough for about ten days after a cold, no fever. Should I be doing anything?",
          "answer": "Your dry cough for about ten days after a cold and without fever needs routine care. It is common for cough to linger after a cold, and this can last up to two to three weeks. According to guidance, a cough alone persisting beyond a cold's duration is expected and does not necessarily indicate a bacterial infection or treatment failure (source: Upper respiratory infection).\n\nWhile your situation is not classified as urgent, it is important to monitor your symptoms. If your cough worsens, becomes new or unexplained, or if you develop other concerning signs, consider discussing this with a clinician. Potential conditions to mention include upper respiratory infections or simply post-viral cough, which can occur after a cold (sources: Upper respiratory infection, Influenza).\n\nStay aware of any severe symptoms or changes that could suggest more urgent concerns, and feel free to seek care if you're uncertain.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
